# Abstract (submission version)

In multi-story fulfillment warehouses, autonomous mobile robots (AMRs) are floor-confined and share a few freight elevators, making elevator capacity the primary bottleneck. Wave composition (which orders to release together) largely fixes makespan before any AMR moves, yet has never been treated as a decision variable coupled to shared vertical transport. We formalize this wave-release coordination problem, fix operational dispatch, and group waves by vertical spread, directional imbalance, and temporal clustering. Two tools using this grouping answer two operator questions. The Bound-and-Gap decomposition answers what wave composition is worth, splitting it into a structural ceiling group-level policies cannot recover and a recoverable slack equal to a predict-then-optimize loss. A resolution theorem shows finer grouping can only raise the ceiling. The Model-Dominance Hedge Rule determines which structure to release when the elevator model is unknown by selecting the one best under the most conservative model. Wherever it is slowest for a wave, this simple hedge provably minimizes worst-case makespan and equals a calibrated, distributionally robust optimum. This per-wave dominance has only two failure channels. A pre-registered 106,800-simulation study tests both tools. The Hedge Rule passes all four pre-registered gates: dominance holds in 99.2% of matched waves in the six-configuration block, and its selection matches that optimum in all six. The decomposition identities hold exactly (72/72), with a partial resolution pass where gaps are small. The capacity-versus-policy verdict is seed-stable, unanimous in 15/18 configurations under the pre-registered measurement. The tools inform operators when composing waves pays and when capacity is the lever.
